🔹 1. Kafka Fundamentals (quick answers)

Q: What is Apache Kafka?
A: Distributed event streaming platform — durable, append-only log for high-throughput publish/subscribe and stream processing.

Q: Why do we use Kafka?
A: Async decoupling, high throughput, fault tolerance (replication), horizontal scale (partitions), message replay (retention), many independent consumer groups.

Q: Main components of Kafka architecture?
A: Producer, Broker, Topic (+ Partitions), Consumer, Consumer Group, Cluster; metadata via KRaft (modern) or ZooKeeper (legacy).

Q: What is a Topic?
A: Named stream/category where messages are published (e.g. `orders`, `payments`).

Q: What is a Partition?
A: Ordered slice of a topic (append-only log). Enables parallelism. Ordering guaranteed **per partition**.

Q: What is an Offset?
A: Sequential id of a message inside a partition. Consumers commit offsets to resume after restart. Tracked per **group + partition**.

Q: What is a Broker?
A: A Kafka server process that stores partition data and serves produce/consume requests.

Q: What is a Kafka Cluster?
A: Multiple brokers working together for scale + HA. (Same idea as Q7 examples above.)

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 2. Producer & Consumer (quick answers)

Q: What is a Kafka Producer?
A: App that publishes records to topics (optionally with a key for partition affinity/ordering).

Q: What is a Kafka Consumer?
A: App that reads records from topic partitions (usually pull-based).

Q: What is a Consumer Group?
A: Set of consumers sharing a `groupId`. Partitions divided among them (load share). Different groups each get a full copy (fan-out). See partition examples above.

Q: How does Producer send messages?
A: Serialize → choose partition (key hash / round-robin / custom) → send to partition **leader** → wait per `acks` setting.

Q: Role of Consumer Offset?
A: Bookmark of progress. After crash/restart, resume from last **committed** offset (may reprocess if crash before commit → at-least-once). See Q2 above.

Q: What happens when a consumer goes down?
A: Group **rebalances**; partitions move to other members; continue from committed offsets. See rebalance section above.

Q: What is Consumer Rebalancing?
A: Redistributing partitions when members join/leave/fail. See detailed examples above.

Q: How to handle duplicate messages?
A: Assume at-least-once. Make consumer **idempotent** (eventId / unique constraint / upsert). Optionally idempotent producer + transactions for EOS.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 3. Architecture & Performance (quick answers)

Q: What is Replication?
A: Each partition has multiple replicas on different brokers (RF=3 → 3 copies).

Q: Leader and Follower?
A: Leader handles reads/writes for a partition; followers replicate. On leader failure, ISR follower becomes leader.

Q: What is ISR (In-Sync Replicas)?
A: Replicas caught up with the leader. `acks=all` waits for ISR acks (with `min.insync.replicas`).

Q: Kafka vs RabbitMQ?
A: See file `RabbitMQ vs Kafka`. Kafka = durable event log, replay, huge throughput, many consumer groups.

Q: How does Kafka achieve high throughput?
A: Sequential disk writes, batching, compression, partition parallelism, efficient networking / zero-copy style transfers.

Q: How does Kafka guarantee message ordering?
A: Only within a partition — use same key (see Q5 above).

Q: What is Retention Policy?
A: Keep data by time/size even after consume (`retention.ms`) — enables replay (see Q6).

Q: What is Log Compaction?
A: Keep **latest value per key** (changelog / current-state topics). Different from time retention.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 4. Advanced Concepts (quick answers)

Q: What is acks?
A: `0` fire-and-forget | `1` leader wrote | `all` leader + ISR (safest with min.insync.replicas).

Q: at-most-once / at-least-once / exactly-once?
A: See table in section 7️⃣ above. Prefer at-least-once + idempotent consumers in most microservices.

Q: Idempotent Producer?
A: `enable.idempotence=true` — broker dedupes producer retries (PID + seq). Does not alone make whole business EOS.

Q: Kafka Transaction?
A: Atomic multi-partition produces; used for exactly-once read-process-write pipelines.

Q: Dead Letter Topic (DLT)?
A: Topic for messages that fail after retries — inspect/replay later.

Q: Kafka Connect?
A: Source/sink connectors (DB → Kafka → ES/S3) without custom producer/consumer apps.

Q: Kafka Streams?
A: Java library for stream processing (map/filter/join/aggregate) inside your app.

Q: Schema Registry?
A: Stores Avro/Protobuf/JSON schemas + compatibility rules so events can evolve safely.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 5. Kafka with Spring Boot (quick answers)

Q: How integrate with Spring Boot?
A: Usually `spring-kafka`: `KafkaTemplate` + `@KafkaListener`. Also raw clients / Spring Cloud Stream — see `different ways to integrate Kafka with Spring`.

Q: @KafkaListener and KafkaTemplate?
A: Template = produce/send. `@KafkaListener` = consume method.

Q: Configure producer/consumer?
A: `spring.kafka.bootstrap-servers`, consumer `group-id` / deserializers, producer `acks` / serializers / `enable.idempotence`.

Q: Consumer exceptions / retries / failed messages?
A: Spring `DefaultErrorHandler` + backoff retries → DLT after max attempts; alert on DLT/lag; idempotent processing.

Q: Monitor Consumer Lag?
A: Lag = latest − committed offset (see lag section above). Prometheus/Burrow/Cloud metrics + `kafka-consumer-groups.sh`.

Q: Reliable MS communication?
A: Contracts (+ schema registry), partition keys, acks=all + RF, idempotent consumers, retries+DLT, lag monitoring, outbox for DB+produce consistency.

